{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And Elohim said: Let there be lights</b>. The reason He said <b>יְהִי</b> in the singular concerning two is that by this He alluded that one of them is what illuminates by day, and therefore He did not use the plural form. And through His speaking in this manner, that which happened came about in the ultimate outcome of the matter — that the moon was diminished, as they of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 60b); for if HaShem had opened His words with ״יִהְיוּ מְאוֹרוֹת״, the matter would necessarily have stood thus, for the word of our Elohim shall stand forever." "<b>Further</b>, it appears we may raise yet another point: He said <b>to divide between the day and between the night</b>. If the lights are equal, and one serves by day and one serves by night, by what would night be distinguished from day, since night would illuminate like day?" "<b>Indeed</b>, the verse intends this: since on the first day He had already created the light — and you find that in the first section He said ״אוֹר״ five times — and they of blessed memory alluded (Tikkunim 5) that five lights were created, and HaShem set them aside as an inheritance for the time to come for the righteous; now, on the fourth day, He emanated from those lights a portion sufficient for the world and suspended them in the firmament of the heavens. And this is what He said: <b>יְהִי מְאֹרֹת</b> — meaning, let there be one portion from the lights created on the first day. And He stipulated that they not be fixed but rather that they revolve, so that through the revolving, day and night would be recognized. And this He did as a sign for the recognition of the consecrated days, as He said: <b>for signs and for appointed seasons</b>, etc. And when the light was emanated, it was emanated in two parts, and both were created to serve the function of day. Therefore you find (Ḥullin 60b) that the moon argued: it is impossible for two kings to use one crown — meaning, at one time. And thus it is brought in the words of our Sages: if the boundary of the moon were at night and that of the sun by day, it would not be one crown for both, but each one's boundary would be on its own. And likewise you find that He said ״וְהָיוּ לִמְאוֹרֹת בִּרְקִיעַ הַשָּׁמַיִם לְהָאִיר עַל הָאָרֶץ״ without fixing a time."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:55:44.970967005Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}